@incollection{ngTraumaticGlaucoma2026,
  title = {Traumatic {{Glaucoma}}},
  booktitle = {{{StatPearls}}},
  author = {Ng, Jonathan K. and Kaur, Kirandeep},
  date = {2026},
  eprint = {36251842},
  eprinttype = {pubmed},
  publisher = {StatPearls Publishing},
  location = {Treasure Island (FL)},
  url = {http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585060/},
  urldate = {2026-07-24},
  abstract = {Traumatic glaucoma is a type of secondary glaucoma occurring because of various mechanisms related to the trauma of the eye. Eye trauma can be divided into 2 categories: closed-globe injuries and open-globe injuries.~All types of ocular trauma can cause an elevation in intraocular pressure (IOP) in the affected eye through various mechanisms. Without appropriate treatment, this can lead to irreversible glaucomatous damage to the optic nerve and potentially permanent loss of visual function. Traumatic glaucoma represents a spectrum of secondary glaucomatous disorders that develop following blunt or penetrating ocular trauma.~This condition is characterized by elevated IOP resulting from structural and functional disruption of the aqueous humor outflow pathways, ultimately leading to optic nerve damage if untreated. Ocular trauma remains a major global public health concern, accounting for millions of emergency visits annually and contributing significantly to preventable visual morbidity. Among its long-term sequelae, traumatic glaucoma is particularly insidious because it may manifest immediately after injury or develop months to decades later, often when the inciting trauma has been forgotten. The delayed nature of presentation underscores the importance of early identification, lifelong surveillance in high-risk individuals, and comprehensive interprofessional management. The pathogenesis of traumatic glaucoma is multifactorial and depends on the nature, mechanism, and severity of injury. Blunt trauma, the most common cause, results in anteroposterior compression and equatorial expansion of the globe. This sudden deformation can damage multiple anterior segment structures, including the trabecular meshwork, Schlemm's canal, ciliary body, iris, and lens. Angle recession is one of the most well-recognized mechanisms, characterized by a tear between the longitudinal and circular muscle fibers of the ciliary body. Although angle recession itself does not always lead to glaucoma, extensive damage—typically involving more than 180° angle—significantly increases the lifetime risk of elevated IOP. The resultant trabecular dysfunction may lead to chronic outflow impairment and progressive optic neuropathy years after the initial trauma. Penetrating injuries, on the other hand, may cause traumatic glaucoma through direct structural disruption, inflammation, intraocular foreign bodies, or scarring of the anterior chamber angle. Surgical repair of globe injuries can further alter aqueous dynamics. Additionally, retained intraocular foreign bodies, particularly those containing iron or copper, may cause toxic reactions (siderosis or chalcosis), leading to progressive trabecular damage and secondary glaucoma. In the acute setting, elevated IOP may arise from transient mechanisms such as hyphema, inflammation, or lens displacement. Hyphema—the accumulation of blood in the anterior chamber—is a common consequence of blunt trauma and may obstruct the trabecular meshwork directly or through red blood cell (RBC) breakdown products. Rebleeding, which often occurs within the first week after injury, can exacerbate IOP elevation and increase the risk of optic nerve damage. Inflammatory debris, fibrin, and cellular infiltrates may also impair aqueous outflow during the acute phase. Prompt recognition and management of these mechanisms are essential to prevent permanent sequelae. Lens-related mechanisms constitute another important pathway. Traumatic cataract formation, lens subluxation, or dislocation can alter anterior chamber anatomy and compromise aqueous drainage. Phacolytic and phacoanaphylactic responses may also occur, particularly with capsular disruption, resulting in secondary inflammatory glaucoma. Similarly, ghost cell glaucoma may develop weeks after vitreous hemorrhage, as degenerated erythrocytes migrate into the anterior chamber and obstruct trabecular outflow. The clinical course of traumatic glaucoma is highly variable. Acute elevation of IOP may present with ocular pain, redness, blurred vision, headache, or nausea, particularly in severe cases. However, chronic traumatic glaucoma—most commonly angle recession glaucoma—may remain asymptomatic until significant optic nerve damage has occurred. This silent progression highlights the importance of thorough initial evaluation and long-term follow-up after ocular injury, even in patients who appear stable at presentation. Epidemiologically, traumatic glaucoma accounts for a meaningful proportion of secondary glaucomas worldwide. The incidence varies based on geographic region, occupational risk factors, and access to protective measures. Young males are disproportionately affected due to higher rates of sports-related injuries, occupational hazards, and interpersonal violence. In pediatric populations, traumatic glaucoma may have unique presentations and requires careful monitoring due to the potential for amblyopia and long-term visual impairment. Diagnostic evaluation begins with a detailed history of the injury, including mechanism, timing, and associated symptoms. Comprehensive ophthalmic examination is essential and includes measurement of IOP, slit-lamp biomicroscopy, gonioscopy, dilated fundus examination, and optic nerve assessment. Gonioscopy is particularly critical for identifying angle recession, peripheral anterior synechiae, or angle-closure components. Anterior segment imaging modalities such as ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM) and anterior segment optical coherence tomography (OCT) may provide additional structural detail in selected cases. Visual field testing and OCT of the retinal nerve fiber layer are indispensable tools for detecting and monitoring glaucomatous damage. Management strategies depend on the underlying mechanism and disease stage. In acute cases, medical therapy aims to reduce IOP and control inflammation while minimizing complications such as rebleeding in hyphema. Topical beta-blockers, alpha-agonists, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are commonly employed, while caution is exercised with prostaglandin analogs in the presence of active inflammation. Corticosteroids play a pivotal role in controlling inflammatory components. Surgical intervention, including anterior chamber washout, trabeculectomy, glaucoma drainage devices, or minimally invasive glaucoma surgery, may be required in refractory cases. Long-term management necessitates ongoing surveillance, as angle recession glaucoma may manifest years after trauma. Preventive strategies remain critical in reducing the burden of traumatic glaucoma. Public health initiatives that promote eye protection in high-risk occupations and sports, enforce safety regulations, and educate people on the importance of~prompt medical evaluation after ocular injury are essential components of prevention. Early identification of high-risk individuals and structured follow-up protocols significantly improve visual outcomes. The interprofessional approach to traumatic glaucoma is fundamental to optimizing patient care. Emergency physicians often serve as the first point of contact and must recognize warning signs of elevated IOP. Ophthalmologists play a central role in diagnosis, monitoring, and surgical intervention. Primary care clinicians contribute to systemic evaluation and long-term follow-up, whereas nurses and allied healthcare professionals support patient education and medication adherence. Coordination among these healthcare providers enhances timely intervention, reduces complications, and improves overall outcomes. In summary, traumatic glaucoma is a complex and potentially delayed consequence of ocular injury that requires vigilance, comprehensive evaluation, and long-term monitoring. The condition's multifactorial pathophysiology—encompassing angle recession, hyphema, inflammation, lens disruption, and scarring—demands individualized management strategies tailored to the mechanism of injury. Early recognition and interprofessional collaboration are critical in preventing irreversible optic neuropathy and preserving vision. As ocular trauma continues to represent a significant cause of visual morbidity worldwide, heightened awareness and structured surveillance remain indispensable in mitigating the long-term impact of traumatic glaucoma. In this review, glaucoma and ocular hypertension, ie, those without glaucomatous optic disc changes,~are collectively known as traumatic glaucoma to not only facilitate readability but also because optic nerve examination and visual field testing,~two of the main modalities to diagnose glaucoma and distinguish it from ocular hypertension, can be challenging to perform in patients with ocular trauma.},
  langid = {english},
  file = {/Users/dewabenny/Zotero/storage/NBZZ84PK/NBK585060.html}
}

